<nav class="seg topseg" aria-label="About"><a class="on" href="/about/how" aria-current="page">How it is made</a><a href="/about/formats">Formats</a></nav>

<article class="prose">
  <!-- The page in five lines, and a way into it: it is long, and most readers want one part (round fifty-eight). -->
  <section class="brief" aria-labelledby="brief-h">
    <h2 id="brief-h">In brief</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Each species page is built ahead of time from public data (GBIF, Kew's WCVP, CHELSA, ETOPO, NASA POWER, iNaturalist, Wikimedia Commons, Wikipedia, OpenAlex), and every figure names its source.</li>
      <li>Nothing about a species is written for its page by a person or by AI, apart from credited quotations: a sentence is a quoted passage, a figure with its source, or a labelled reading of a fixed rule, from templates written once for every species (<a href="#written">who wrote them</a>).</li>
      <li>A source that did not answer is said as "not checked" (a refusal as refused), and a call this site held back, or a source a build skipped, as "not asked"; never as "none".</li>
      <li>Your own plants are kept in this browser; their records leave it only through sync, encrypted with a key only you hold, or a backup you download (a name you type or import may be looked up in the catalogue, as below).</li>
      <li>No accounts, no analytics, no third-party scripts; the code is open.</li>
    </ul>
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#sources">Sources</a> · <a href="#list">Which species</a> · <a href="#refusal">Refusals</a> · <a href="#records">Records</a> · <a href="#marker">Map marker</a> · <a href="#climate">Climate across the range</a> · <a href="#year">Seasons</a> · <a href="#floor">Cold floor</a> · <a href="#archetypes">Archetypes</a> · <a href="#notes">The figures at the top</a> · <a href="#related">Similar climate</a> · <a href="#collection">Your collection</a> · <a href="#privacy">Privacy</a> · <a href="#glossary">Glossary</a> · <a href="#source">Source code</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
  <h2 id="sources">What comes from where</h2>
  <p>A species page is assembled from public sources, each named on the page where its data appears, with the licence it came under. The name and its synonyms, family and order come from the <b>GBIF Backbone Taxonomy</b>; a name the backbone holds as a synonym is followed to the species it accepts (and an accepted subspecies or variety one step further, to its species), and the page says which name was asked for. The common name shown is one of the English names GBIF reports, chosen by a fixed rule, never by hand, and not the name growers use most. Where a source gives several names in one entry, separated by commas ("Sago palm, King sago, Sago cycad, Japanese sago palm"), each is read as a name of its own, given by that source. Spellings of one name (case, hyphens, spaces, apostrophes) count as one name, given by as many sources as give any of them, each source counted once. Names are put in order by a fixed rule: a name GBIF marks preferred first, then the name more of GBIF's sources give, then GBIF's own order; but a genus named on its own, as a single word, comes after every longer name, so it is shown only when no source gives the species a fuller name (for Aloe vera a longer name, such as "Barbados aloe", is shown, and "Aloe" stays among its names); and a list of several names separated by semicolons goes after every other name, and so does another genus of the reference named alone ("Osteospermum" for Dimorphotheca jucunda) or as the last word of a longer name ("Flatleaf Senecio" and "String-of-Beads Senecio" for a Curio), unless that genus is one English uses as a noun of its own (agave, aloe, amaryllis, cereus, crocus, haworthia, iris, lotus, mimosa and yucca: "Lace aloe", "Autumn crocus" and "Zebra haworthia" stay, and "Lotus" for Nelumbo nucifera stays among its names), and so does the species' older name ("Senecio rowleyanus" for Curio rowleyanus). Capitals are not read, so a name is set back or kept however its source wrote it; a genus word that is not the last ("Arum lily") is not read, and the species' own genus is read only when it is the whole name ("Aloe"). Of a name's spellings, the one more sources give is shown, then GBIF's first, except that spellings differing only in capitals count together: of those, the one shown is a source's spelling with the capitals some source gave on purpose and no others (a capital counts as meant when its spelling leaves another word in lower case, as "herb Robert"; Title Case and all-lower-case spellings say nothing), so "Butterfly milkweed" is shown over "Butterfly Milkweed" however many lists give the second. The first letter is shown as a capital, and nothing else of the source's spelling is changed. GBIF's preferred flag and the count of sources exist only for species whose names were fetched again by the build's <code>--names</code> step, which asks GBIF again for every species; for the rest, each source a listed spelling names counts once, and a spelling that names none counts as one. The species page and the catalogue use the same rule. The species page shows the first four names under the name, separated by semicolons, and every English name is searched. The native range comes from the <b>World Checklist of Vascular Plants</b> at Kew, read from Kew's files; a name WCVP lists more than once (a homonym) is told apart by authorship, and when authorship does not decide, the page says the name is unresolved and attaches no range. Regions where WCVP records the plant as extinct are shown labelled as such and are never part of the range records are tested against. Where WCVP has no entry the app falls back to national checklists and says so, at country level only. Kew's own one-line descriptions of a species, its life form and climate class, are quoted on the page and credited to WCVP, RBG Kew; nothing is derived from them. Occurrence records come from <b>GBIF</b>. Monthly climate normals come from <b>CHELSA V2.1</b> (1981–2010, 30 arc-second), packed to a 0.05° grid: day and night temperature, rainfall, solar radiation, relative humidity, vapour-pressure deficit and wind. Elevation comes from <b>ETOPO 2022</b> on the same grid. Daily temperature extremes come from <b>NASA POWER</b> (1981 onward; each page states how many years its series covers). Photographs come first from the media that <b>GBIF</b>'s occurrence download carries (research-grade iNaturalist observations reach GBIF this way, and so do other observation datasets), then from <b>iNaturalist</b> directly for the cultivated set and for species the download gave few of, then from <b>Wikimedia Commons</b>; every photograph is restricted by its own licence to CC0, CC BY and CC BY-SA, whatever dataset it came through, and each carries its licence, and its author where the source names one (a CC0 photograph without one says so; under CC BY and CC BY-SA a photograph without an author is not published). On a species page and on the compare page each photograph shows its author and licence; a small tile elsewhere (the front page, the catalogue, Related) gives the licence and the author's name, cut to forty characters, as the index carries them ("Photo: CC BY, Jane Doe"), and names the site the photograph came from instead where the index carries no credit: where the credit cannot be said short and true (no licence, an author's line naming another licence, or a line in no form the build writes), and in an index built before October 2026; the species page it opens gives the full credit and a link to the source. A Commons photograph is loaded at its 800-pixel version, never the original upload; one whose page holds no such version is not shown. A credit whose author's line names one licence and whose source tags another says the two disagree. The summary is the opening of <b>Wikipedia</b>'s article, four sentences at most, quoted as written, kept visibly separate and credited, with a link where more followed. Recent literature comes from <b>OpenAlex</b>.</p>
  <h2 id="list">Which species are here</h2>
  <p>The species list was derived, not chosen by hand: every accepted species, as WCVP lists them, of 173 collector genera (the cacti, the mesembs, the stapeliads and caudiciforms, the winter-growing bulbs and their like; the list is <code>scripts/specialist-genera.txt</code> in the source), plus the 2,500 species most often recorded as cultivated on iNaturalist across every kind of grower, resolved to accepted names, less the cereal, turf and timber families. The very large genera are not taken whole, and only the species of theirs that reached the cultivated count are here: Aloe (9 species, of the several hundred WCVP accepts), Euphorbia, Senecio, Oxalis, Dioscorea, Peperomia, Hoya, Ceropegia, Ornithogalum, Gladiolus, Moraea and Pelargonium. So a species that is not here is not a statement about the species, only about the list, and the list can be extended by rebuilding with more names (the README says how).</p>
  <p id="feature">The front page's photographs and its one species shown whole are chosen by rule, not by hand: of the 48 genera with the most species that have eight photographs or more and a habitat climate, the species of each with the most openly licensed records; twelve of those 48 are shown a day, in turn, and the first of the day's twelve is the species shown whole, on a wide screen, when its page has a habitat climate. On a phone the strip of these photographs follows the first rows of the catalogue.</p>
  <p>The catalogue's search reads a query in turn. First as a whole, against the common names, so "Black eyed Susan" is the black-eyed Susans however it is capitalised. When that finds nothing, as a botanical name written the way growers write it: with var., subsp., f., fo. or v.; with cv., after which the rest is a cultivar and is left out; with "cf.", "cfr.", "aff.", "vel aff.", "nr." or "near" (between two words of a name), which are skipped; with "sp.", "spp." or "nov.", which end the name; with "x" or "×" for a hybrid, after which the name starts again, so the word after the sign is never an author; with a cultivar in quotes after the name, which is left out when it begins with a capital (quotes around the whole query are taken off, and the text inside is searched); or with an author citation pasted after it, in either case ("L.", "l.", "Britton &amp; Rose"). A capitalised word is read as an author only after an epithet of four letters or more, and a word ending in a full stop, or a capitalised abbreviation of four characters or fewer ("St.", "L."), only after an epithet or a rank, so "Lily of St. James" and "String of pearls." are searched whole. When nothing matches exactly, one typing error is allowed per word of four letters or more, against the species' names only (its name, its common names and its older names), never against its family or the places it grows, which are matched only as written; after a first word with a capital, a capitalised word is matched only against a whole word, so "Aloe Verra" is Aloe vera, not a species that grows in Algeria, and "Echeveria Lola" is not Echeveria lilacina. Then the query is tried again on its first two words (a hyphenated epithet is one word, a hybrid formula is tried on its first parent, and a single letter is never tried again). Last, when still nothing matches, a genus followed by capitalised words (a cultivar without quotes, "Crassula Gollum") is answered with its genus, and only when the first word is a genus of the reference, so a common name in title case ("St Johns Wort") is never cut to its first word. Whenever words were left out, the page says so: "Searched for “Copiapoa cinerea”, leaving out “Phil.”" when the name matched and an author, a cultivar or a word in another script was set aside, and "Nothing matched … as written. Showing results for …" when the query was tried again; when the matches came by a similar spelling, it says so.</p>

  <!-- The claim made precise, and the templates' authorship said once and plainly (round sixty-one; visitor 11). -->
  <p id="written">Nothing about a species is written for its page by a person or by AI, apart from credited quotations. Every sentence on a species page is a quoted passage with its credit, a figure with its source, or a labelled reading of a fixed rule: the figures at the top, the season card and the cultivation sheet are figures with their sources and the readings two fixed rules make of them, and each section's "How this section is made" says which figure each sentence rests on. The templates those sentences are filled into, the labels and the notices (such as the line saying no photograph is on file) were written once, the same for every species, by the author with Claude, an AI model, as the code was; they are in the source (<code>src/lib/core/sheet.ts</code> and the page files), and no text is written or generated for any one species. The labels over the figures ("Cold floor (1 night in 100)", "Warmest month, mean daily high", "Rain a year (median across the range)", "Open-sky light"; on a page built before October 2026, "Rain a year (sum of monthly medians)") are the names of the figures under them, not readings of them. No row says what a plant does, wants or tolerates, or what to do to it.</p>

  <h2 id="refusal">A refusal is not an absence</h2>
  <p>Sources rate-limit, time out and go down. When one does, the page says "not checked" for that section rather than showing an empty list, because "we could not ask" and "there is nothing" are different facts and a grower reading the page needs to know which. Wherever there is no room for the sentence, the mark is one dashed pill reading "not checked", the same on every page, and tapping it says which source, and when. A source that refused is said to have refused, one that failed or was silent to have not answered, and a call this site held back, or a source a build did not ask, is "not asked". The corpus build keeps what a source gave last time when it refuses this time, and records the date it was carried from. What is carried is exact: literature, the summary and photographs, each per source, so a refused iNaturalist does not discard the Commons photographs the same build got; and the range, the records, the map marker and the climate as one snapshot when the occurrence or distribution source refuses, never piecemeal, because a range from one build and records from another would be a page no build made. A species page whose stored data cannot be read at the moment it is asked for says the page could not be read just now, and no cache keeps that answer; it is never said to be absent. On Today, when the species sheets do not answer (refused, unreachable, or still running after fifteen seconds), every plant past its rhythm is listed and each stop says "Resting months not checked"; the sheets are asked for again on "Check again" and at the next visit. The same rule applies in the other direction: a species with no habitat climate on file gets no derived climate rows, and a hybrid gets none at all, since it has no habitat.</p>

  <h2 id="records">Which records count</h2>
  <p>A species' records on GBIF include herbarium sheets with rounded coordinates, garden plants, and specimens filed under the wrong country. The app takes every georeferenced record with no flagged geospatial issue, drops living specimens (something somebody planted) and records marked introduced or managed, and tests each against the native range from WCVP, discarding records outside it. The range has to be WCVP's, with native status stated: a national checklist that only reports a plant present in a country cannot tell a wild record from a garden one, so for a name WCVP does not carry the page shows the records on a map, says the range is unverified, and derives no marker, no climate and no sheet from them. Restricted-licence records (CC BY-NC) inform the marker and the climate like any other; only openly licensed records are published on the map, in the data, or as the marker itself. Three agreeing records are enough for a narrow endemic; under a dozen the page says the evidence is thin.</p>

  <h2 id="marker">The map marker</h2>
  <p>The marker on the map is where the records are densest, and it decides nothing else. Records are binned at 1°, and the bin with most neighbours wins if it holds at least half the records or twice the runner-up; if no bin dominates at 1° the test repeats at 2° and 4°, and the densest 4° block stands when none dominates even there. Inside the winning cluster the densest 1° population is found again, and the marker is the openly licensed record nearest its middle, so the pin is somewhere the plant has been seen. When no openly licensed record lies in the population, the marker is the centre of the nearest tenth-of-a-degree cell instead, so that a restricted record's coordinates are never published; the page says which kind of point it is. The climate is not read at the marker.</p>

  <!-- The glossary's plain words through this page: "across the range", "a typical spot in the range", "plant", "sync key", "hash group", reading the log rather than folding it, and "this version of the app" (round fifty-eight; the accessibility review). -->
  <h2 id="climate">The climate across the range</h2>
  <p>The habitat climate is read across every in-range record, not at one point. Each record placed to within 10 km (or, with no stated uncertainty, a person's observation with coordinates to three decimals, the way a phone records them; a herbarium sheet placed at a locality's centre with no figure, and every vaguer record, stays on the map and off the climate) names one 0.05° cell of the packed CHELSA grid, and each distinct cell is read once, so a site visited a hundred times is one cell of climate and not a hundred votes. A cell at sea or on ice is dropped and counted: land is the grid's elevation layer, which averages only land pixels, so a cell with no land in it has no elevation (CHELSA itself carries temperatures over the ocean, so figures alone are no test); the few basins whose whole cell lies below sea level read as sea. A species recorded in strength on both sides of the equator (three or more cells poleward of 10° on the smaller side, a fifth of the cells that far from the line) takes its year from the larger side alone, since a January median over both is a month no place has; cells within 10° of the equator, where the hemisphere rule does not apply, stay in either way, and the page says what was left out. Three land cells are the minimum; fewer, and the page says so and derives nothing. For each month and each variable the app takes the median across those cells, which is the year the sheet reads, and the 10th and 90th percentiles, which are the span the species is recorded in; the page draws both, the median as a line and the span as a band around it, on a climograph (temperature over rain, one twelve-month axis, its legend saying the band is across habitat cells and not across years), and prints them in a table beneath, stating how many cells and records the figures across the range rest on. The chart is drawn in the habitat's own months, and says so above it with the habitat's hemisphere. It shades the three months around the coldest month's mean nightly low as the cold quarter, which is a marker for the eye and not a rule the sheet uses, and marks the extremes at its edge with their source, NASA POWER. The rain total across cells is the 10th and 90th percentile of each cell's own annual total, never a sum of monthly percentiles, which would be a year no cell has. Radiation is CHELSA's daily-mean shortwave flux, taken to a daily total by the 86,400 seconds in a day and converted to a daily light integral (PAR taken as 45% of shortwave at 4.6 µmol per joule, so 1 MJ/m²/day ≈ 2.07 mol/m²/day), because that is the unit a grower can compare with a window or a lamp.</p>
  <p>Extremes need daily data, which CHELSA does not give, and one series, so they are read at one cell, a typical spot in the range: the one whose coldest month's mean nightly low is nearest the median of that figure across the range (monthly means are what the grid holds; the coldest single night is a POWER figure and is read there afterwards). That typical spot is the cell nearest that median whose NASA POWER cell (0.5° of latitude by 0.625° of longitude) is mostly land by the same elevation layer; a cell on a coast or an island is averaged with the sea, so up to eleven further candidates, each within 2 °C of the median, are tried, and when none is mostly land the nearest stands and the page says what fraction of its POWER cell is land. The position printed for that cell is the cell's centre, never a record's coordinate. The app fetches the NASA POWER daily series for that cell's POWER cell and reduces it: the absolute minimum, the 1st-percentile night, the 99th-percentile day, and frost nights as a count over the whole record, so one frost in forty years is printed as one night, not rounded away. POWER's cell elevation and the grid cell's ETOPO elevation differ, so the series is lapse-corrected at 6.5 °C per 1,000 m of the difference, and the page says by how much; when the grid cell's mean elevation is below sea level (a coastal cell averaged with sea floor), or when no elevation is on file for either cell, no correction is applied, and the page says which. The figures record a correction of 0 m and no correction alike, so the Temperature card says "0 m or none" for either; the extremes' source line, under Evidence used, says which. A series is used only if it covers twenty years at 95% completeness.</p>

  <h2 id="year">What the rules read</h2>
  <p>Two fixed rules read the median year, and the sheet labels every sentence they produce as a reading. The rain rule: the wet season is the smallest set of months that carries 70% of the year's rain; a wet season whose mean is more than half a degree below the year's is called a winter growing season, more than half a degree above a summer one, and within that band neither. When 70% of the rain takes eight months or more the rule reads no short rainy season: the rain is spread, which says nothing against a long one. A temperature curve that moves less than 4 °C between its warmest and coldest month is flat, and then the rule infers no growing season even from a sharp rainy season; the page says the rain is sharp and that nothing is inferred from it, which is a different statement from "no season". Under 120 mm a year there is no rainy season to read at all, and the temperature rule names the cooler six months instead, provided they are at least a degree cooler than the other six; a flat year names nothing. A season that falls in two runs of months is printed as two runs. None of this says when the plant grows.</p>
  <p>Wherever habitat months are printed the page names the hemisphere they belong to and, where the shift rule applies (the temperature curve moves 4 °C or more between its warmest and coldest month, and the habitat lies 10° or more from the equator), gives them shifted six months for a collection in the other one, because a Namaqualand plant whose rain falls in May to August has that season in a northern greenhouse from November to February, and the difference is the whole point. The label line and the sheet format a season through one function, so they cannot disagree. The season card at the top of a species page draws the same reading as twelve months in the reader's calendar: the months the rule names shaded, the months under 5 mm of rain marked, nothing shaded for a spread year or a year with nothing to read. The reader's hemisphere is their site's, else that of their first place with coordinates, else the northern, and the card says which.</p>

  <h2 id="floor">The cold floor</h2>
  <p>The floor on a sheet is the 1st-percentile night at a typical spot in the range, from NASA POWER. When no extremes are on file (none read, the source refused or not asked, or read at a weather cell that is mostly sea and so not used), no floor is read: the coldest month's mean nightly low in the median year from CHELSA is shown in its place, named as what it is, with a line saying it is not a floor, since a month's mean of its lows is warmer than the nights a floor is read from; a label prints no habitat night then. The archetype table's figure for a group (below) is never a floor and never changes one: it is shown apart, named as a convention with no source. A floor is a figure about a place, not a tested survival limit for a plant in a pot, and the page says so.</p>

  <h2 id="archetypes">Archetypes</h2>
  <p>The app keeps a small table of care groups (cactus or succulent; bulb, tuber or caudex; tropical foliage plant; orchid; epiphyte; fern or moss; carnivorous plant; temperate garden plant), resolved species → genus → family, and the page says which group and why. For four groups the table holds one figure, the lowest temperature the group is conventionally kept at indoors: tropical foliage plant 12 °C, orchid 13 °C, epiphyte 10 °C, fern or moss 5 °C. No source is given for these figures: they are the author's statement of a growers' convention, and the page shows one only as that, apart from the habitat's figures, and never lets it raise or replace a cold floor (an older version took the higher of the two as the floor, and gave a frost-hardy Puya 10 °C over a habitat night of −3 °C). The other four groups span too much for one figure and carry none. The table supplies no prose. Terrestrial and frost-hardy genera are kept out of the tropical groups: the terrestrial bromeliads (Dyckia, Puya, Hechtia, Deuterocohnia, Encholirium, Fascicularia and their like) and the terrestrial or temperate orchids (Bletilla, Pleione, Disa, Habenaria, Cypripedium, Calanthe, Dactylorhiza, Ophrys, Paphiopedilum, Phragmipedium, Phaius, Ludisia, Cynorkis, Cymbidium and their like) are in no group at all, nor are the rock and ground bromeliads Alcantarea and Billbergia, or Selaginella. The page names the group as the table's listing ("listed under the genus Copiapoa in the archetype table"), not as a claim about the plant. A family that splits between groups (Bromeliaceae, Orchidaceae, Araceae and the like) assigns no group by the family alone: only a genus or species on the table does, and where neither is, the app guesses nothing.</p>

  <h2 id="notes">The figures at the top</h2>
  <p>The top of a species page, headed "Habitat figures", states each figure once, under its own name and with its source as a small tag beside it: the cold floor (1 night in 100), with the record low over the same years printed beside it, both at a typical spot in the range, from NASA POWER, or, when no floor is read, the coldest month's mean nightly low, named as that; the warmest month's mean daily high; the rain a year, the median of the range's grid cells' own yearly totals (each cell's twelve months added first, then the middle of those totals taken, or the halfway point between the two middle totals when the cells are even in number; a page built before October 2026 has no such median and shows the twelve monthly medians added, named as that), and how many months bring 25 mm or more; and the open-sky light, the DLI of the darkest and brightest months; the rain and the light each have a bar drawn on a fixed scale, said under it and to a screen reader (full at 1,200 mm a year, and at 70 DLI for the brightest month); all but the cold floor and the record low are from CHELSA, and all of those but the rain a year from the median year across the range. The chart draws the median of each month, and its description gives their total as that, the twelve monthly medians added; the growing-season rules read the same median year, since "70% of the year's rain" must be a share of the months it adds up, so the rain rule's total can differ from the figure at the top. The cultivation sheet's Rain line and the season reading give the median year's total instead (the twelve monthly medians added, which the rain rule reads), named "in the median year"; where the page has the cells' median, the Rain line gives both and says which is which. Where two or more months tie for a figure as it is printed, every one of them is named. Then the season card. The cards further down hold the full sentences and the rules, each closed until opened. A plant's printed label carries three of these in one line: the season, the cold floor (only when the daily extremes are on file, as "floor 6.5 °C (1 in 100, NASA POWER)") and the open-sky light ("open sky 30–65 DLI (CHELSA)"); for a species with no habitat figure at all it carries the group's convention instead, marked "convention, no source".</p>
  <p>The same discipline holds on a plant's own page, where its habitat figures sit beside the place it lives in: for each compared figure the page shows the habitat median with its 10th–90th span and the place's setting side by side, and gives no verdict, because the sky over a habitat and a climate percentile are facts about places, not measured tolerances of a plant in a pot. The papers listed on a species page are works whose title or abstract names the species, most cited first; they are a pointer to the literature, and nothing on the page is drawn from them.</p>

  <h2 id="related">Similar habitat climate</h2>
  <p>Near the foot of a species page, Related lists up to six species whose habitat climate is nearest its own. The distance is one number: the root of the mean squared difference, month by month, in mean daily high and mean nightly low temperature, plus the difference in rain on a log scale weighted so that a factor of 2.7 in a month's rain counts as three degrees. Months are compared in calendar order, so a habitat with the same seasons six months apart is far, not near: on a bench in one hemisphere, two such plants want opposite halves of the year. Nothing else enters, not range, not family, not photographs, and the list is computed once when the index is built, from the same figures across the range the pages show. It is a statement about climates, not about plants: two species from alike climates can want different things in a pot, and the page says which figures were compared.</p>

  <p id="corpus">The reference is built on a PC and uploaded as files: one data file per species page, and beside them the things the server would otherwise work out from the index for every request, worked out once at build time: the index itself, the index entries of each hash group, each group's figure sheets, the search's postings (which entries each short key of a name can match) and the catalogue's rows for each grouping and chip. Each of these products is stored under the hash of its own content, so a file is never rewritten, no cache can hold one file's body under another's name, and a refresh uploads only the files whose content changed. A manifest names every current product by its hash, and the server reads the manifest first; a manifest whose files are not all there is not adopted. The corpus id is a hash of the index, so the same index is the same id on every build. A search reads only the postings its words need and the entries they point to; the server holds the index in memory and reads the rest by file, which is what lets the reference grow past what one server process could hold. The sitemap dates each species page by the day its data last changed in what the page shows, so a refresh that changes nothing does not move it; a genus row takes the latest of its species' days; an index built before October 2026 carries no such day, and its pages are dated by the day the reference was built.</p>

  <h2 id="collection">Your collection</h2>
  <p>Everything you record lives in your browser's storage on your device as an append-only log of field-level changes, each stamped with a hybrid logical clock. What the app shows is the log read through from the start (kept as a snapshot between loads, so opening the app does not read every change again; <a href="/about/formats#snapshot">the formats page</a> says when the snapshot is dropped); two devices merge by taking the union of their logs, and for each field the latest change wins. That one rule is what backup restore and sync both use, which is why restoring an old backup over a newer collection loses nothing and why a sync interrupted halfway simply resumes. Sync encrypts every sync bundle (a sealed bundle of changes) on the device with a key only you hold; the server stores ciphertext and cannot read a plant name. What the server does hold is a token derived from the key (it cannot be turned back into the key), sent with every request as the bearer; so the token is visible wherever request headers are, the platform included, and whoever holds it can read the ciphertext, write junk into the vault up to its limit, and take a photograph's name before your upload does, but cannot open anything. The key itself sits in this browser's storage in the clear, as the collection does, so anyone with this browser profile has both; there is no rotation and no revocation yet: "Stop syncing" forgets the key on this device and revokes nothing on the server, and a key you believe exposed means a new vault. Sync is also trust in the server's listing: a bundle the server withholds, or an edit it makes to the listing, is not something a device can detect, since bundles carry no chain; what it can detect is a bundle altered in transit (the seal fails) and a photograph swapped (its hash is in the record). The <a href="/about/formats">formats page</a> has the details.</p>

  <h2 id="privacy">What the site knows about you</h2>
  <ul class="plain">
    <li>No accounts, no analytics, no tracking, no third-party scripts.</li>
    <li>Your collection is stored in this browser; it leaves only through sync, encrypted on the device, or a backup file you download.</li>
    <li>Your plant pages ask the server for the reference's figures by hash group (a few hundred species each), never by name.</li>
    <li>The frost watch sends the coordinates of your site and of any place that has them (rounded to about a kilometre, with an altitude if one is set) and your units to this server, which asks the weather services. Frost shows on Today and in the frost line under the top bar on every page; no notification is sent, because sending one would mean the server keeping a push address and a location for each device.</li>
    <li>A search typed in the catalogue or on the compare page goes to the server. The species picker sends the name you are typing to the server twice: as typed to the catalogue's search, which the server answers from its own index, as it answers the catalogue's search box; and only its species part (the genus as typed and the epithet in lower case, never a qualifier, a rank and what follows it, a cultivar, an author or a field number) to the name service, which the server passes on to GBIF. When that species is not found and the name is a genus followed by capitalised words, the genus alone is asked too. A search of your own plants stays on the device. The names in a list you import are checked by hash group, then by the catalogue's search, never by GBIF's name service.</li>
    <li>Two small cookies: your units, and once a site is set, its hemisphere.</li>
    <li>When a page loads, the app's service worker asks this site whether a new version is out (one conditional request for <code>/service-worker.js</code>, once each time a page is loaded in full; moving between pages inside the app does not repeat it). When a move between pages fails or ends on an error page, the framework asks once for <code>/_app/version.json</code>, to learn whether a new version is out.</li>
    <li>The server keeps short-lived rate counters by address, dropped within two days, and nothing about your plants. Cloudflare's edge keeps copies of public answers, linked to no address: a forecast for a rounded site for an hour (five minutes when the National Weather Service did not answer for its alerts); a catalogue search, a name typed into the species picker (in lower case, with GBIF's answer), an old-name check of a species address and a hash group's figure sheets for a day; and the front page and species pages as rendered, for a minute.</li>
    <li>The server also caps how often it asks the weather services and GBIF: each of MET Norway, the National Weather Service and GBIF has a share of 600 calls a minute for every visitor together, and one address (an IPv4 address or an IPv6 /64) may take a tenth of a share in a minute, and an IPv4 /24 or an IPv6 /48 four tenths; a US forecast takes from both MET Norway's share and the NWS's, both or neither. The last quarter of each share is kept, every minute, for networks that have not yet called that service in it: once three quarters are used, a network that has already made two calls to it that minute is held back, and one that has made fewer may make up to two. So ten addresses can no longer spend a share; it takes at least 77 different networks in one minute. That is the limit that remains: visitors spread over that many networks can still spend a share for the rest of a minute, since a share is 600 calls by design. Past the cap a forecast or a name is not asked, and the page says the site held the call back rather than that the service did not answer: "this site's calls are used up for this minute" when the share is spent; "the rest of this site's calls for this minute are kept for visitors on networks that have not called yet" when only the reserve is left and this network has had its two calls; and "this address has used its part" or "this network has used its part" for an address or a network past its part (the species page's line about GBIF says only that this site held its call back for this minute, which covers all four). A network here is a block of addresses (an IPv4 /24 or an IPv6 /48), which a mobile carrier, a university or a shared building connection may give to many people at once: a grower on such a network can be held back for calls others on it made, and is told so, never that the service did not answer. The same holds for uploads: the 12 GB a day an IPv6 /48 may store is shared by every household its provider numbers inside it. The reserve is kept by the server's counter object; if that does not answer, each server process counts the shares and the parts itself, sharing its counts through KV once a second, and keeps no reserve.</li>
    <li>Species pages, the front page and the compare page load photographs from iNaturalist (two hosts), Wikimedia Commons and GBIF's image cache, which see your address; your own pages do not unless you switch that on.</li>
  </ul>
  <details class="full"><summary>Full detail</summary>
  <p>There are no accounts, no analytics and no tracking. Species pages are served from a static corpus. Your collection, meaning its records (names, numbers, notes, dates, places, photographs), is stored in this browser and is sent nowhere unless you turn on sync, and then it is sent encrypted. What the pages about your own plants do send this site's server, and it is this list and nothing else: when any page loads, the service worker's check for a new version of the app (one conditional request for <code>/service-worker.js</code>, once each time a page is loaded in full; the framework's own five-minute version poll is turned off; a page is reloaded under a new version only when an older version was serving it and the version taking over is another, which the page asks the worker itself, inside the browser, so a first visit and a second copy of the same version are never reloaded under you); the hash group of each species you grow when a page needs the reference's figures (the catalogue is split into a few hundred species a group, 32 groups at its present size and more as it grows, so a species is narrowed to one in a few hundred and no further; <a href="/about/formats#privacy">the formats page</a> gives the rule); the coordinates of your site and of any place with coordinates when the frost watch asks for a forecast (<code>/api/forecast</code>), with the place's altitude if one is set, and your units, so the answer comes back in them (rounded on the device, to a hundredth of a degree, about a kilometre, and to ten metres, before they leave it; once a site is set, the watch asks from any page: when a page opens or is returned to, every five minutes while one is in view, and at once when the site is changed in Settings; an answer is kept half an hour on the device (in this tab's session storage; five minutes when the National Weather Service did not answer for its alerts) and the same site is not asked again until it is that old, so the frost line can show under the top bar on every tab; a refused request, or no answer within ten seconds, is not kept, and is said as a check that did not happen); two small cookies, your units and, once a site is set, one letter for which hemisphere it is in, which goes only with species and compare pages so they render your months the right way up before any script runs; the name you are typing in the species picker, sent twice: as typed to <code>/api/search</code>, which the server answers from its own index, and only its species part (the genus as typed and the epithet in lower case; never a qualifier, a rank and what follows it, a cultivar, an author or a field number) to <code>/api/names</code>, which the server passes to GBIF's name service (a cross is asked about by its genus alone, and only of the name service; when no species of the name is found and it is a genus followed by capitalised words, the genus alone is asked too); the names in a list you import that the hash groups do not settle, each asked of the catalogue's search as the front page asks it (never of GBIF's name service); the hash group of the species a scanned plant label names, when this device holds no plant by that label's identity, so the page can show that species, as a plant page asks for its own (the label's species is never sent by name); the text you type into the catalogue's search box on the front page or the search on the compare page, which the server answers from its index (the whole index used to come to the browser for that, and no longer does), and which is the same thing a species page you then open already shows; not the text you type on "Your species", which is searched on the device, nor anything shaped like a plant number, nor anything typed before your collection has opened; the position of the catalogue's rows you scroll to or jump to by letter (a grouping and a row number, nothing of yours), which the server answers from its index; a species address the reference does not answer to, which the server asks the backbone about once, in case it is an older name for a species that is there (the answer is kept at the edge for a day); a request for the reference's current version (<code>/api/corpus</code>), once per page, which carries nothing but the request; after a move between pages that fails or ends on an error page, the framework's one request for <code>/_app/version.json</code> (the app's version, to learn whether a reload is needed; nothing of yours); and, when you follow a link to a species page, that species, since you opened it (links on these pages are not preloaded on hover, so a name goes only when you click). No page address is sent as a referrer with any request, so a plant's number or a search never rides along with one. Those pages ask no outside host for anything unless you switch the reference's photographs on for them, in Settings or with the offer shown where a photograph is withheld, in which case the photograph is fetched from the image host named below, which then sees that this address asked for that species' picture. Your browser does talk to outside image hosts on species pages, and you should know which: photographs on species pages, on the front page (its strip and the catalogue's rows) and on the compare page load from iNaturalist's image hosts (inaturalist-open-data.s3.amazonaws.com and static.inaturalist.org), Wikimedia Commons (upload.wikimedia.org, at the 800-pixel size) and, for photographs that reached GBIF from other observation datasets, GBIF's image cache (api.gbif.org), each of which sees your address like any image host (a photograph that reached GBIF from another dataset is shown from GBIF's cache, not from that dataset's own server); and the species picker sends the name you are typing to this site's own <code>/api/search</code>, and its species part to <code>/api/names</code>, which asks api.gbif.org from the server, so that host sees the server's address and not yours. The frost watch asks MET Norway (api.met.no) and, in the US, the National Weather Service (api.weather.gov) from the server, never from your browser; a name the reference does not answer to is asked of GBIF (api.gbif.org) from the server too. Those three are the only hosts the server asks, and your browser asks no host but this site and the four image hosts ("Use my location" asks the browser for its position; how the browser finds it is the browser's own, and the site sends it nowhere but as above). The site's three typefaces are among its own files; until they arrive, a page is set in faces the device already has (Segoe UI, Georgia and Consolas on Windows; Helvetica Neue, Georgia and Menlo on Apple devices; Liberation and DejaVu on Linux), each scaled to the same average width, so the text hardly moves when the typefaces land. Using a device's own font is no request. The one thing the server keeps about a visitor is short-lived rate counters keyed by address (an IPv4 address or an IPv6 /64, and for vault creation, sync and its batches and photographs, searches, reference reads, the index and the sheets among them, and the requests that call another service, the /48 around it too, and for the calls to other services themselves an IPv4 address's /24 too): requests per ten minutes in KV, calls to other services per minute in the counter object's memory (never stored; without the counter object, in KV for two minutes), and bytes stored per day and vaults created per day in a small counter object; every address key is dropped within two days of the day it was written (at the UTC midnight that ends the following day), the request windows a running Worker holds in memory are dropped as later requests arrive, each within ten minutes of its last use, and none holds a page, a name or a record. Cloudflare's edge, in front of the server, keeps copies of public answers so the services behind them are not asked twice: a forecast for an hour (five minutes when the National Weather Service did not answer for its alerts, so they are asked again soon), keyed by the rounded coordinates and altitude it was asked for (the same rounding the device applies before sending, so a site's forecast is kept there under its rounded position, with nothing else), a catalogue search for a day, keyed by the corpus, the number of answers asked for and the query, its case kept; a name typed into the species picker for a day, keyed by the name in lower case, with GBIF's answer to it; a species address the backbone was asked about for a day; a hash group's figure sheets for a day, keyed by the group and the corpus; and the front page and each species page as rendered, for a minute, keyed by the build, the reference's version, the address, the queries the page reads, the units and (on a species page) the hemisphere letter. None of these holds an address, a plant or anything of a grower's own. With sync on, the server also keeps each vault's stored size beside the vault and in the counter object, for the storage allowance, with a note of each removed object for two days so its size is given back once, and when each photograph's name was last claimed by an upload, for two days, so a removal asked for before a newer upload leaves that upload alone; and the count of vaults that hold something, with each such vault's id (the random name your key derives, which the server holds anyway) and the day of its last upload, so a vault is counted once and a place left unused for 90 days is given back (without the counter object, KV keeps the same as <code>vaultplace:&lt;id&gt;</code>); a small pointer beside the vault for each photograph removed, stored again after a removal, or uploaded again while it was already there (a retry after a lost reply, an Undo) since the server began keeping them: after a removal, the removal's receipt (its proof, its time and the generation it removes), kept so the removal stands and a removal cut off partway is finished when the device asks again, and otherwise which generation holds the photograph; for a vault whose place was given back after 90 days, when that was, in the vault's own record and in the counter object, until an upload takes a place again; while a removal is deleting, a note of it in the counter object for up to ten minutes, so the vault's size is not put right from a listing made halfway; for each photograph a removal, or a storing after a removal, has touched, a mark in the vault's counter object (the photograph's id and when it was marked), until the midnight sweep finds nothing of that photograph left unnamed, or has been unable to read it on seven nights; and, for a moment while its midnight sweep is cut short, the name of the last key it read (<code>s:&lt;prefix&gt;</code>), deleted once the sweep finishes. A write to a vault from a page on another site is refused. Every sync request carries the header <code>X-Cultifolio-Sync: 1</code>, which a page on another site cannot send, so this site's own sync writes from a browser that does not say which site a request came from (Safari before 16.4, which sends <code>Origin: null</code>) are still let through. A photograph you remove is removed from the server too: once the removal is ten minutes old (past any Undo), a device asks the server to drop the sealed bytes, and the request carries a proof that only a holder of the sync key can make, so the bearer token alone, which can add to a vault, can never destroy in it. If another device's upload of that photograph lands after the removal, the server keeps it for up to two days (the upload's claim on its name) and the device asks again later, at most hourly; the bytes go once that claim has lapsed, unless the photograph was brought back. A photograph brought back after the server removed its bytes is stored again only by a device with the sync key. What the device keeps outside the collection, every key: in the browser's local storage, <code>cultifolio.frost.site</code> (your site's coordinates and name, as you set them; "Use my location" keeps three decimals; they are rounded further only when a forecast is asked for), <code>cultifolio.prefs</code> (your settings), <code>cultifolio.theme</code> (light or dark), <code>cultifolio.compare</code> (the species in your comparison; in the example collection, that tab's own copy, below), <code>cultifolio.corpus</code> (the reference's version last seen), <code>cultifolio.labels</code> (the label sheet's choices), <code>cultifolio.lastLocation</code> and <code>cultifolio.lastSowLocation</code> (the place last used on the Add and sowing forms), <code>cultifolio.countMode</code> (how a batch page last took its counts), <code>cultifolio.visits</code>, <code>cultifolio.lastVisit</code> and <code>cultifolio.installSnoozedUntil</code> (when to offer installing the app), <code>cultifolio.welcomed</code> (the front page's welcome dismissed), <code>cultifolio.hasMine</code> (that this device has species of its own, so the front page opens on them; never written from the example collection), <code>cultifolio.persistAfterFirst</code> (that the browser was asked, after your first plant, to keep this site's storage, and its answer said) and <code>cultifolio.persistAskedAt</code> (when a page last asked it again; a page asks at most once a month, and only once there is something of yours to keep, never for a visitor or in the example collection; Safari and Chrome answer by themselves, Firefox asks you, and no page waits for your answer), and <code>cultifolio.clockOffsetMs</code> and <code>cultifolio.clockPending</code> (a correction to the device's clock, read from the sync server's <code>Date</code> header, and one waiting for its second reading; the formats page has the rule); in this tab's session storage, <code>cultifolio.forecast</code> (the frost watch's answers, for half an hour, or five minutes when the National Weather Service did not answer for the alerts), <code>cultifolio.demo</code> (the example collection is open in this tab), <code>cultifolio.demo.</code> and a setting's name (that setting, for the example's tab only, such as <code>cultifolio.demo.units</code> or <code>cultifolio.demo.compare</code>), <code>cultifolio.labelsPicked</code> (the plants picked on the labels page, written when the page is left and read back only when that page is reloaded, so a reload keeps them), <code>cultifolio.sampleLeft</code> (this tab has just left the example collection, so its next page deletes the example's database), <code>cultifolio.sampleClosed</code> (this tab was sent home because the example closed in another tab, so the next page says so), <code>cultifolio.sampleOut</code> (this tab has left the example collection, so an empty Today, Places or Propagation offers it rather than opening it again), <code>cultifolio.freshKey</code> (a sync key just made, so a reload still shows it), <code>cultifolio.backupNudgeHidden</code> (the line suggesting a backup put away for this tab), <code>cultifolio.iosFirstHidden</code> (the iPhone and iPad card about the Home Screen put away for this tab) and <code>storage-notice-hidden</code> (the plants list's storage notice dismissed); in IndexedDB, the collection itself (<code>cultifolio</code>; <code>cultifolio-demo</code> for the example collection, deleted by the first page after you leave it or, when another tab still has it open, as that tab closes, which the page says; a browser without Web Locks cannot tell an open sample from a left one, so there one left by a tab closed without leaving is kept; <code>cultifolio-staging</code> while a backup is being restored); in the cache storage, the app's own files and the species pages you have opened, for offline use (<code>cultifolio-</code> and the build's version), and the hash groups' entries and sheets your pages asked for, kept by the reference's version so a new build of the app does not fetch them again (<code>cultifolio-corpus</code>); and, while a page is open, names the browser holds for coordination and nothing else: the channel <code>cultifolio-vault</code> (<code>cultifolio-demo-vault</code> in the example), on which a tab tells this browser's other tabs that it wrote to the collection, so they read it again; the lock <code>cultifolio-sync:</code> and the vault id, so two tabs never sync at once; the lock <code>cultifolio-import</code>, so two tabs never write an import at once; and in the example collection the locks <code>cultifolio-sample-open</code> and <code>cultifolio-sample-seed</code> and the channel <code>cultifolio-sample</code> (<a href="/about/formats#sample">the formats page</a> says what they do). And one path does reach the server: the address of a plant's page (<code>/plants/2026-0001</code>) is fetched from the server on the first visit after the app is installed, and again if the browser's offline cache is cleared; after that the page is served from the device without a request. The server does not log paths; the number is never in a referrer.</p>
  </details>

  <h2 id="glossary">Words the app uses</h2>
  <p><b>Plant</b>: one plant (or one pot of a clump) under its own permanent number, the plant number; the number is never reused, even after the plant is gone. <b>Batch</b>: a sowing or a tray of cuttings, numbered like a plant, from which plants are potted up. <b>Place</b>: a room, bench, shelf or tray, nested, with the conditions a plant inherits from it. <b>Vault</b>: the encrypted copy of your collection on this site's server, which sync keeps; it holds sealed bundles of changes and photographs the server cannot read. <b>Sync key</b>: the thirty symbols that encrypt the vault and let a second device join it; there is no account behind it and no recovery. <b>Sync bundle</b>: a sealed bundle of changes one device sends, as the sync page names it (the formats page, describing the wire, calls it a log batch); also the unit a device keeps whole when it cannot read one. <b>Could not be read here</b>: a sync bundle this version of the app could not read (usually from a newer version), kept whole on the sync page for a later version to read; nothing in it is lost. <b>Waiting</b>: a record on this device that lacks a field this version of the app requires (a name, a status), usually because its sync bundle could not be read here or the file it came from never had the field; it is kept, not shown, and completes when the field arrives. <b>Not checked</b>: a source that did not answer when a page was built (it refused or failed; a refusal is said as refused); not the same as a source that answered "none". <b>Not asked</b>: a source the build skipped, or a call this site held back because its calls were used up for the minute; not "none" either. <b>Pending</b>: the habitat climate has not been derived yet; not a refusal. <b>Held</b>: a change dated more than five minutes ahead of this device's clock, kept out of the collection until the clock reaches it, then counted by itself. <b>Parked</b>: a change from a clock that was badly wrong (stamped more than two days past the arrival of the sync bundle that carried it), kept in the log but counted only if you apply it, on every device alike. <b>Not reached</b>: this site could not be reached from the device just now; not the same as "not in the reference". <b>Species page</b>: the reference's page for one species, built ahead of time from the sources above. <b>Across the range</b>: across every cell of the climate grid that holds the species' in-range records; a figure across the range is the median of those cells, with the 10th to 90th percentile span. <b>A typical spot in the range</b>: the one cell whose coldest month's mean nightly low is nearest the median across the range; the daily extremes, which need one weather series, are read there. <b>Day and night</b>: on the chart and in the table, CHELSA's monthly means of the daily maximum and the daily minimum; a month's "mean daily high" and "mean nightly low" are these, not the month's mean temperature. <b>Cold floor</b>: the 1st-percentile night at a typical spot in the range, from NASA POWER, so one night in a hundred is colder; <a href="#floor">the cold floor</a> above has how it is read and what stands in when none is. <b>Convention</b>: the care groups table's figure for a group, the lowest temperature it is conventionally kept at indoors; it has no source and is never a habitat figure or a floor. <b>Habitat months</b>: months as they fall where the species grows wild, in its hemisphere; the chart is drawn in them, and the season card turns them into yours. <b>DLI</b> (daily light integral): the light a day delivers, in moles of photosynthetically active photons per square metre; the habitat's is CHELSA's shortwave radiation for the open sky, converted at 2.07 mol of PAR per MJ, and says nothing of the shade a plant grows in.</p>

  <h2 id="source">The source</h2>
  <p>Cultifolio is open source: the app under AGPL-3.0, the corpus tooling under MIT. Everything described on this page can be checked, run and rebuilt from <a href="https://github.com/zomethingje-eng/cultifolio">github.com/zomethingje-eng/cultifolio</a>, including the scripts that build a species corpus from the sources above. Problems and corrections are welcome there.</p>
